    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Most property claim disputes are not about whether a storm happened. They are about the number: how many roof squares, whether siding can be repaired or must be replaced, what local labor costs. <strong>Insurance appraisal in San Marcos</strong> is a process written into most Texas property policies for resolving that kind of disagreement over the amount of loss. As a San Marcos public adjuster, we help policyholders decide whether appraisal fits their dispute and prepare the documentation it depends on. Joseph Dittman, our licensed public adjuster, is also a certified insurance appraiser and has served as an insurance umpire, so we understand how panels review evidence.</p>

<h2>How the Appraisal Clause Works</h2>
<p>Wording varies by policy, but most appraisal clauses follow the same basic structure:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Written demand.</strong> Either party demands appraisal in writing after a disagreement on the amount of loss.</li>
<li><strong>Each side names an appraiser.</strong> Each appraiser is expected to be competent and impartial, usually within a set number of days.</li>
<li><strong>Appraisers select an umpire.</strong> If they cannot agree, either side can usually ask a court to appoint one.</li>
<li><strong>Appraisers evaluate the loss.</strong> They inspect, review estimates and documentation, and try to agree on the amount.</li>
<li><strong>Umpire resolves differences.</strong> Items the appraisers cannot agree on go to the umpire.</li>
<li><strong>Award.</strong> A written award signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss, as the policy provides.</li>
</ol>

<h2>When Appraisal Fits, and When It Does Not</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Appraisal may fit?</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Insurer accepted the roof claim but priced it far below local bids</td><td>Often</td><td>Pricing and scope are amount-of-loss questions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Disagreement over repair vs. replacement of matching materials</td><td>Often, depending on policy wording</td><td>How much it costs to restore the property is usually an amount question</td></tr>
<tr><td>Disagreement over the number of roof squares or damaged elevations</td><td>Often</td><td>Quantity of damage goes to the amount of loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>Claim fully denied as wear and tear</td><td>Usually not by itself</td><td>Whether the loss is covered is a coverage question</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flood exclusion applied to water damage</td><td>Usually not</td><td>Exclusion disputes are coverage issues; consult a Texas attorney</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insurer has not finished its investigation</td><td>Usually premature</td><td>There must be an actual disagreement on the amount</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Causation can blur the line. If the insurer agrees there was hail damage but disputes whether certain slopes were hit, that may be treated as an amount question in some cases and a coverage question in others. Texas courts have addressed this issue, and the answer depends on the facts and policy wording. Public adjusters do not give legal advice, so a Texas attorney is the right person to ask how it applies to your claim.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/appraisal-dispute-review-table.webp" alt="Professionals reviewing roof damage photos at a table with a gavel and scales" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Appraisal panels decide the amount of loss based on the evidence each side presents.</figcaption></figure>

[[cta]]

<h2>Appraisal vs. Other Ways to Resolve a Dispute</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What it addresses</th><th>Typical cost and time</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Supplement or reconsideration request</td><td>Missing items, quantities, pricing, new evidence</td><td>Lowest cost; weeks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Appraisal</td><td>The amount of loss when the parties disagree</td><td>Appraiser and shared umpire fees; weeks to months</td></tr>
<tr><td>TDI complaint</td><td>Claim handling concerns; asks the insurer to respond to the regulator</td><td>No fee; does not decide the claim</td></tr>
<tr><td>Litigation</td><td>Coverage disputes, bad faith, and contract claims</td><td>Highest cost; often many months or longer; requires an attorney</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Weather-related lawsuits in Texas involve pre-suit notice requirements under Insurance Code Chapter 542A, and many policies limit the time to file suit. A Texas attorney can explain how those rules interact with an appraisal on your claim.</p>

<h2>Pros and Cons of Appraisal</h2>
<p><strong>Potential advantages:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Usually faster and less expensive than litigation.</li>
<li>Decided by people who understand construction and estimating.</li>
<li>Focused on the evidence about cost and scope.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Potential drawbacks:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>You pay your own appraiser and share umpire costs.</li>
<li>It generally does not resolve coverage disputes.</li>
<li>Under most clauses the award is binding as to amount, so a weak file can lock in a low number.</li>
<li>The insurer may still raise policy defenses after the award.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Preparing a Strong Appraisal File</h2>
<p>An appraisal is only as good as the evidence behind it. Before demanding appraisal, make sure you have:</p>
<ul>
<li>A complete, line-item estimate for the full scope of damage, preferably in Xactimate so it can be compared directly. See <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a>.</li>
<li>Photos and measurements of every damaged area, including close-ups and overview shots.</li>
<li>Storm data for the date of loss. In Hays County, NOAA records large-hail events such as <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181409" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2.5-inch hail near the San Marcos outlet mall on May 9, 2024</a>. Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a> can help.</li>
<li>Local itemized contractor bids and supplier quotes for specialty materials.</li>
<li>Code requirements and permit information for the City of San Marcos or your city, plus your ordinance or law coverage limits. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a>.</li>
<li>Your full policy, declarations page, and all claim correspondence.</li>
</ul>
<p>Appraisal is often a later step. Many disputes are resolved earlier through a well-documented <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplement</a> or reconsideration request on an <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid claim</a>. We usually recommend exhausting those options first.</p>

<h2>What Happens After the Award</h2>
<p>Once an award is signed, the insurer typically issues payment based on the award amount, minus the deductible, prior payments, and any depreciation that applies under the policy. If the award separates replacement cost from actual cash value, recoverable depreciation may still depend on completing repairs. The insurer may also continue to assert coverage positions it reserved during the process. Review the award and the payment letter together, and ask questions about anything that does not add up.</p>

<h2>Our Role as Your San Marcos Public Adjuster</h2>
<p>When we represent you as your public adjuster, we help you assess whether the dispute is really about amount, build the estimate and evidence the appraiser will rely on, and help you identify qualified, impartial appraisers in Central Texas. Because policies generally require an impartial appraiser, a public adjuster with a fee interest in your claim typically should not also be your appraiser on that same claim.</p>
<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Appraisal does not guarantee a higher payment. If you are weighing appraisal on a claim in San Marcos, Hays County, or a nearby community, a licensed <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> can review the dispute with you. Request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
